In article <1990Oct27.185529.11359@midway.uchicago.edu>
 goer@quads.uchicago.edu (Richard L. Goerwitz) writes:

[deletions]

>There are all kinds of scenarios that could be worked up in this
>manner using real spells in Hebrew, Akkadian, Phoenician, Greek,
>Egyptian, Latin, Aramaic, and other such ancient languages.
>
>It would just give everything more of a fun spirit if the incantations
>were real, and the mistakes had some more logical consequences.


This is fine as long as no one in the group has studied these
languages.  As a doctoral candidate in Classics (Greek, Latin)
I find that this is, however, very disappointing for me in any RPG.  
Even when it's in a language I don't know, I find that instead of
inspiring me to think of "magical lore" or "mystical incantation"
I think of how the game designer is trying to build his fantasy
world with things that are *to him* other-worldly, where in fact
they are "this-worldly."

The worst thing, though, that I see done in RPG's is improper handling
of the original languages.  I've seen too many misspellings, incorrect
grammar, and historical inaccuracies to really enjoy what good stuff
there is out there.  I would rather the game designer use words that
were utter nonsense than dabble in a few phrases from a real,
historical language.


